.TH DETACH 1 "2021-07-04" "detach-process" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
detach \- spawn a new process and detach it from the current session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B detach
.RI [OPTIONS] " -- " program " [ARGS...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B detach
utility launches a program in a separate session so it continues running
independently of the interactive shell.
It forks, starts a new session, and forks again so the launched program is
not a session leader.
Standard input is redirected to
.IR /dev/null ,
and standard output and standard error are redirected to temporary files in
.IR /tmp .
The
.RB \-\-
argument separates detach options from the command to run and is required
when options are used.
The parent process displays the program PID and the paths used for standard
output and standard error, then terminates.
.PP
A regular input file must differ from the files selected for standard output
and standard error. The PID file must differ from all three streams.
These checks include hard links and symbolic links and run before existing
file contents are changed. Devices such as
.I /dev/null
may be shared by the input and output streams.
.PP
Executable scripts should include an interpreter line such as
.B #!/bin/sh
or be launched explicitly with their interpreter.
Automatic shell execution without an interpreter line depends on the host
system and is unavailable with musl.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show this help message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-pid FILE
Write the launched program's process ID to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B \-\-stdin FILE
Read the standard input from
.IR FILE
instead of
.IR /dev/null .
.TP
.B \-\-stdout FILE
Write the standard output to
.IR FILE
instead of a temporary file.
.TP
.B \-\-stderr FILE
Write the standard error to
.IR FILE
instead of a temporary file.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options. Required when options are used.
.SH OUTPUT
On success, the parent process outputs:
.TP
.B PID:
The process ID of the launched program.
.TP
.B stdout:
The file used to capture the child's standard output.
.TP
.B stderr:
The file used to capture the child's standard error output.
.TP
.B pid:
When
.B \-\-pid
is provided, the PID is also written to the specified file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No startup error was reported.
.TP
.B > 0
An error was encountered.
.PP
The exit status describes startup, not the program's eventual exit status.
An abrupt termination before exec may close the startup channel without
reporting an error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a ping command detached from the current session:
.PP
.RS
detach -- ping -c 10 google.com
.RE
.PP
Write the PID and the standard output and error to specific files:
.PP
.RS
detach --pid /tmp/ping.pid --stdout /tmp/ping.out --stderr /tmp/ping.err --
ping -c 10 google.com
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/stdout.XXXXXX
A temporary file for standard output generated by
.BR mkstemp (3)
when
.B \-\-stdout
is not provided.
.TP
.I /tmp/stderr.XXXXXX
A temporary file for standard error generated by
.BR mkstemp (3)
when
.B \-\-stderr
is not provided.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkstemp (3),
.BR setsid (2),
.BR fork (2),
.BR execvp (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Vladislav Punko <iam.vlad.punko@gmail.com>
